output.text:
{"connection_id": "BC-25bb37b4e3a6073127d1", "ref": "Didache 1:1", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two ways of life and death", "note": "The commentary prays for the path of the graced and distinguishes it from the astray; the Didache opens by naming two ways, one of life and one of death, giving an early Christian ethical frame for that choice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8b0e553d51d560e95dfa", "ref": "John Chrysostom, Homilies on Matthew 61", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Matthean debt as sins before the heavenly King", "note": "In his homily on Matthew 18:23–35, Chrysostom reads the enormous debt as sins forgiven by the heavenly King and the fellow servant's debt as an interpersonal claim. This interpretation makes the parable's monetary reckoning a moral account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3e66a418130771478a8c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.18", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "ransom beyond silver or gold", "note": "The commentary says a person's offered equivalent cannot settle the final account; Peter says believers were ransomed from futile inherited conduct not with silver or gold, giving a Christian account of redemption whose means differs from a cash equivalent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c84a81f2a672b476b877", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.19", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Christ's costly blood", "note": "The commentary says the balance is not tilted by a substitute offered on judgment day; Peter identifies Christ's precious blood as the ransom price, a Christian atonement reading that locates redemption in another's life rather than the debtor's payment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-03f8ca2ec443d2fdd91d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.4.5", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "account before judge of living and dead", "note": "The commentary's day leaves no one absent from the account; Peter says people will give account to the one ready to judge the living and the dead, tying accountability to the whole human span."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c4fe7ab440605c02ab01", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.4.5", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "account to the judge of living and dead", "note": "Those opposed to believers will give an account to the one ready to judge the living and the dead. It provides the commentary's account, universal judge, and resurrection horizon in one formula."}
{"connection_id": "BC-12d18d6c978e5594a9f6", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "day of the Lord arrives like a thief", "note": "Paul says the day of the Lord comes like a thief in the night. It makes the commentary's day an arriving event that overtakes people rather than an abstract date."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7efe74172a87ec20cef7", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sudden destruction with no escape", "note": "The claim of peace and safety is followed by sudden destruction that cannot be escaped. This gives the prior day-of-the-Lord image its inescapable consequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-727ad9cb665b88e2a28e", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.5.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "each receives for deeds before Christ's tribunal", "note": "The commentary says the day is an individual account with no one absent; Paul says all must appear before Christ's judgment seat to receive for what they did in the body, specifying personal recompense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b41e2e19b06574528108", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.5.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "all appear to receive what is due", "note": "All must appear before Christ's judgment seat to receive what is due for deeds done in the body. It combines the commentary's public standing, account, and equivalent return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-445217b9549d723ce41d", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "leaving the straight way", "note": "The commentary contrasts a straight path with those who stray; Peter says false teachers have left the straight way and followed Balaam's path, giving apostasy a concrete route image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c0488df2d9ffdf46963d", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.3.10", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "day comes; created order passes and works are exposed", "note": "The day of the Lord arrives like a thief, the heavens and elements pass away, and the earth's works are exposed. It enlarges the commentary's day-as-event into a cosmic disclosure of deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d30010977ab377de8b5b", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Thess.1.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "affliction repaid to afflicters", "note": "The commentary glosses din as recompense and anger as punishment; Paul says God repays affliction to those who afflict, making retribution a balancing of harms."}
{"connection_id": "BC-42d404175d7aa947a3aa", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Thess.1.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God repays affliction to afflicters", "note": "God is said to be just in repaying affliction to those who afflict believers. This is a specific final-judgment use of the commentary's reciprocal payment and punitive wrath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-26c9af3c01bead4a8ca7", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Thess.1.9", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "punishment at the Lord's revelation", "note": "The commentary interprets divine anger as punitive consequence; Paul describes punishment when the Lord Jesus is revealed, placing that consequence at an eschatological appearance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-99195ea6d7584276b8ac", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.31", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "appointed day; judge; resurrection", "note": "The commentary calls the day an event that arrives and links it to rising; Paul says God appointed a day to judge the world through the raised man, joining the fixed day, judgment and resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f0a4867659a34e89db1b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.31", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God fixed a day to judge the world", "note": "Paul announces a day fixed for judging the world in righteousness, guaranteed by resurrection. It is a direct day-of-judgment and resurrection formulation alongside Mālik yawm al-dīn."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7e667f49c8afa30450bf", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.25", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wrongdoer repaid without partiality", "note": "The wrongdoer will be paid back for the wrong done, with no partiality. This supplies a concise Christian statement of impartial retribution that matches the commentary's exact-scale logic."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0e39ed6fd542066cc563", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.30", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Deuteronomy's vengeance applied as warning", "note": "The commentary glosses divine anger as punishment and dīn as recompense; Hebrews quotes “vengeance is mine, I will repay” and “the Lord will judge his people” from Deuteronomy to warn its hearers, giving those Hebrew formulas a Christian judgment setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-19ad36c078f254395da9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "make straight paths for feet", "note": "The commentary asks for guidance on a straight path; Hebrews tells its hearers to make straight paths for their feet so the lame may be healed, turning the path image into communal moral practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bfb88fc2aac7dd413d37", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.2.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "every transgression receives just retribution", "note": "Hebrews says every transgression and disobedience received a just retribution. It states the commentary's dīn as measured return in juridical rather than commercial language."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1d074c9a610303c79004", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.4.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no creature hidden; all exposed before God", "note": "Hebrews says no creature is hidden and all are laid bare before the one to whom account is due. It is a close counterpart to the commentary's insistence that no one can evade the day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e718abfa61373b1fc54", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.9.27", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "death followed by judgment", "note": "The commentary makes the day of account universal and unavoidable; Hebrews says people die once and after that comes judgment, adding a concise sequence of death and reckoning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0a3ae7785951dddddd0c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.9.28", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Christ bears sins; appears to save", "note": "The commentary says no offered substitute is accepted on the judgment day; Hebrews says Christ was offered once to bear the sins of many and will appear to save those awaiting him, setting out a Christian mediating account while placing the offering before the second appearance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-047921399e0b402e9c9c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mercy triumphs over judgment", "note": "The commentary recalls that a victim's kin may forgive and that payment should be gracious; James says judgment is without mercy to one who showed no mercy, while mercy triumphs over judgment, linking interpersonal mercy to the final account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11c2d7c480dd5263ee02", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "withheld wages cry out to the Lord of hosts", "note": "The unpaid wages of laborers cry out and their cries reach the Lord of hosts. It transfers the commentary's honest trade and unresolved claim into a social injustice heard by divine authority."}
{"connection_id": "BC-853957fba36d4897865e", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.1.23", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaiah's straight way applied to John", "note": "The commentary's prayer is for guidance on a straight path; John identifies the wilderness voice with Isaiah's call to make the Lord's way straight, interpreting the path as preparation for the Lord's arrival through John the Baptist."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d1bfe32fb4fe28010a20", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.28", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "those in tombs hear the voice", "note": "The commentary's standing scene presupposes the dead are raised; Jesus says all in the tombs will hear his voice, adding a summons from the life-giving judge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-344dbb8a78819f058c4c", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.28", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "all in tombs hear the judge's voice", "note": "Jesus says that all who are in tombs will hear his voice. It supplies the universal summons that precedes the two resurrection outcomes in the following verse and the commentary's standing scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b94bebb87cbd0f937350", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.29", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "resurrection of life or judgment", "note": "The commentary separates the guided from the astray under final judgment; John says those who did good come out to resurrection of life and those who did evil to resurrection of judgment, giving an explicit two-outcome scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc896006bfa644e7780b", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.29", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "resurrection of life and resurrection of judgment", "note": "Those in the tombs come out either to resurrection of life or resurrection of judgment. It gives the commentary's standing resurrection its two-sided outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f9890dbcacce89bfba46", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.11.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sins and debtors in prayer", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the source word dīn from the related deyn debt; Jesus' prayer asks forgiveness of sins while naming those indebted to the speakers, explicitly joining moral fault to debt language."}
{"connection_id": "BC-584adf69abbc4f1fb700", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.11.4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "forgiven sins and forgiven debtors", "note": "Luke's prayer asks forgiveness of sins because the pray-ers forgive everyone indebted to them. It makes the commentary's proximity of debt and recompense into an explicit moral and devotional analogy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc820c850edd04c4e898", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.59", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no release until the last lepton is paid", "note": "Jesus' legal warning ends with payment of the last small coin before release. It provides the commentary's exact-price and due-debt image in a judicial setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef3fc073a6125e1251b7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "steward commanded to render an account", "note": "The steward must give an account of management before losing his position; the following scene concerns debtors and their bills. It supplies an administrative form of the commentary's account-and-debt imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7ecf3b8c9724dac28407", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.5", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "debtor's bill and amount owed to the master", "note": "A steward asks a debtor how much is owed to his master and works from the written bill. This gives the debtors and documents that make the preceding command to render an account concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20e434e562bfa184206c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.19.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "king returns and calls servants to account", "note": "After receiving kingship, the returning ruler summons servants to learn what their trading has gained. Kingship, commercial calculation, and reckoning meet in one parable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b4bdbd6d59dafee97394", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.6.38", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "full, pressed, shaken, overflowing measure returned", "note": "Jesus promises a good measure, pressed down and shaken together, returned into the giver's lap, and adds that the measure used returns to its user. This turns the commentary's full measure into a precise reciprocity formula."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6fb82a4104ef4e27df63", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.7.42", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "creditor forgives two debts", "note": "The commentary distinguishes a due debt from its final repayment; Jesus tells of two debtors unable to repay whom one creditor freely forgives, showing debt as a measure of mercy as well as liability."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ac0e3878e4f58fea2ee", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.10.45", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "the Son of Man's life as ransom", "note": "The commentary invokes a day when no offered substitute can be accepted; Mark calls the Son of Man's life a ransom for many. The shared payment image leads to a specifically Christian resolution rather than a general commercial analogy."}

{"connection_id": "BC-81643847ccbd4f2c28cf", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.12.36", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "account on the day of judgment", "note": "The commentary makes dīn an account and repayment; Jesus says people will give an account for careless words on the day of judgment, extending the reckoning to speech."}
{"connection_id": "BC-126aa8a7442b9367ebb0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.12.36", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "account on the day of judgment", "note": "Jesus says people will render account for every careless word on the day of judgment. The explicit account sharpens the commentary's dīn as reckoning, including what might otherwise be forgotten."}
{"connection_id": "BC-954ff0b8548b845888c5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.16.26", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no exchange for one's life", "note": "The commentary says no equivalent can be offered on that day; Jesus asks what a person could give in exchange for life after gaining the world and losing himself, sharpening the limit on substitution."}
{"connection_id": "BC-160b5a6d91814c6c6230", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.16.26", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "exchange for one's life", "note": "Jesus asks what a person could give in exchange for his life. It is a direct New Testament formulation of the commentary's final limit on offering an equivalent or ransom."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e688df011d77d6d5bff6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.16.27", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Son of Man comes and repays each person", "note": "The commentary joins the arriving day, sovereign rule and recompense; Jesus says the Son of Man comes in the Father's glory and repays each according to conduct, a direct Christian formulation of that combined scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b492141d5e840af2ef6f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.16.27", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "coming judge repays each according to practice", "note": "The verse following the question about an exchange for life says the Son of Man will come and repay each according to practice. It keeps ransom's limit and deed-for-deed recompense together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b1d6b9f153d1f95ac201", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.23", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "king settles accounts", "note": "The commentary joins malik, account and due debt; Jesus' kingdom parable begins with a king settling accounts with his servants, embodying sovereign judgment as creditor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4d1b92ef2414fade9bc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.23", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "king settling accounts with servants", "note": "Jesus likens the kingdom to a king who begins to settle accounts with his servants; the king, reckoning, and unpaid obligation form a direct narrative counterpart to the commentary's ruler of the day of account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d1a82457ab8b6135943d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.24", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unpayable debt", "note": "The commentary's debt image reaches a due reckoning; the first servant owes ten thousand talents and cannot pay, making the account's imbalance concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-27bfb77226d505ae56e0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.27", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "debt released by compassion", "note": "The commentary presents repayment as the day’s governing image; this king instead feels compassion and cancels the servant’s debt, adding mercy as a complication within a royal account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-072bf0f0744eb40025dd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.34", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "debt reinstated; punishment", "note": "The commentary links unpaid obligation with judgment; after the servant refuses mercy, the king hands him over until the debt is paid, joining debt collection to punishment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-050e6fb751fad2d474f9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.34", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "debt exacted under punishment", "note": "In the same parable the master hands the debtor over until the whole debt is paid. It makes the commentary's due debt and punitive recompense a single judicial action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-56aa221035bcb7a9f166", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.31", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the Son of Man sits on a glorious throne", "note": "The commentary calls the day the sovereign's domain; Jesus' judgment scene opens with the Son of Man coming in glory and sitting on his throne, adding the ruler's enthronement before the separation already described nearby."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0b7267917250ba0de1e3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.31", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "royal throne of final judgment", "note": "The Son of Man comes in glory and sits on his glorious throne. This supplies a visibly royal form for the commentary's owner and ruler of the judgment day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4cea6df13190d43a7e62", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.32", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "nations separated before the king", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the graced from the angered and astray; Jesus gathers all nations before the enthroned Son of Man and separates people as a shepherd separates sheep and goats, making the division a judgment scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-08640f9fcfb780747c37", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.32", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "all nations gathered and separated before the throne", "note": "All nations are gathered before the enthroned Son of Man and separated as sheep from goats. It supplies the assembled-humanity action between the royal throne and the divergent final rewards."}
{"connection_id": "BC-807aac588ba6300f870c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.46", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "punishment or life", "note": "The commentary contrasts those under anger with those guided on the favored path; Jesus' judgment ends in eternal punishment for one group and eternal life for the other, giving the separation its final stakes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-70e0e5eb4fee3aaf0a9f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.46", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "eternal punishment and eternal life", "note": "The final separation ends in eternal punishment for one group and eternal life for the other. It states the opposed outcomes implied by the commentary's recompense and divine wrath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c33b9a5ca69c207cd461", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.5.25", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "settle with an accuser before court", "note": "The commentary makes a debt due under judgment its central image; Jesus tells a debtor to settle with his accuser on the way to court before the judge hands him over, adding urgency before a judicial account closes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0bfc533db955102db48f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.5.26", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "last coin owed until paid", "note": "The commentary links a full-weight coin to the debt's final settlement; Jesus says the prisoner will not leave until paying the last quadrans, making even a small coin part of complete collection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2cbfc103653a2d42f74a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.12", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "forgive debts as debtors are forgiven", "note": "Matthew's prayer names moral forgiveness as the mutual release of debts. It gives a New Testament path by which commercial obligation is transposed into accountability before God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c67e36d3dcc385f0eb18", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two gates and opposed ways", "note": "The commentary asks for the straight path and marks out the astray; Jesus contrasts a wide gate and broad way leading to destruction with the narrow way, making moral direction a choice of route and destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-93f03f2636720daeb93c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "constricted way leading to life", "note": "Jesus contrasts the constricted way leading to life with the broad way to destruction. It gives the commentary's straight path and loss a specific New Testament path-outcome contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c251b25f0b465b550a27", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.2", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "measure used becomes measure received", "note": "Jesus says the measure one uses will be measured back. It turns the commentary's exact measure into a reciprocal ethical rule, though it does not portray a literal final balance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2df1d79c1d054de6c229", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phlm.1.18", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "another person's debt charged to Paul", "note": "Paul tells Philemon to charge any wrong or debt owed by Onesimus to Paul. This concrete assumption of another's obligation illuminates the commentary's language of putting one thing in another's place."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f3e28c3522e56f362fc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.11.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "the world's kingdom becomes the Lord's kingdom", "note": "At the seventh trumpet the kingdom of the world becomes the kingdom of God and his Christ, who reigns forever. This gives the commentary's exclusive final sovereignty an apocalyptic Christian formulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d6999a40b41e832a85c3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.11.18", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "time for wrath, judgment, and reward", "note": "The verse announces God's wrath, the time to judge the dead, reward servants, and destroy destroyers. It gives a compact New Testament counterpart to punishment and payment on the decisive day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f5b006996701abc70e1e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.16.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blood given to blood-shedders as their due", "note": "Because the persecutors poured out the blood of saints and prophets, the bowl judgment gives them blood to drink, “for they deserve it.” This makes bloodshed itself the measure of eschatological recompense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9acd41118397a4ed71ae", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.18.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "repay, double, and measure back in the cup", "note": "The heavenly command says to repay Babylon as she repaid, double her works, and mix double in her own cup. It gives the commentary's equivalent return and unbending measure an apocalyptic form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9d18a5cad2ec31597749", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.12", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dead stand; books opened", "note": "The commentary's resurrection scene has creatures standing before the Sustainer; Revelation shows dead, great and small, standing before the throne as books open and they are judged by their works."}
