{"connection_id": "BC-f11e9568b0c24ef03366", "ref": "WLC:Ps.96.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "YHWH comes to judge the earth", "note": "The commentary describes the day as an event that arrives and the balance as just measure; the psalm says YHWH comes to judge the world in righteousness, making arrival and equitable judgment one scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a85676b97bae55967a4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.96.13", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "YHWH comes to judge earth in righteousness", "note": "The psalm announces YHWH's coming to judge the world in righteousness and peoples in faithfulness. Its universal, equitable judgment fills out the commentary's claim that the day is a true accounting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2a91ec0daf3c57ea280c", "ref": "WLC:Zech.14.9", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "YHWH king over all earth “on that day”", "note": "The commentary's day transfers undivided rule to its Mālik; Zechariah says YHWH will be king over all the earth and, on that day, one with one name. It makes royal exclusivity explicitly eschatological."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e54cac6d95fbd8271325", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.1.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the approaching day of the Lord", "note": "The commentary presents yawm as a severe event that arrives; Zephaniah calls the great day of the Lord near and hastening, adding a prophetic day-of-judgment formula."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6ae45af74ded3a0e11ac", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.1.14", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew יום יהוה, “day of the Lord”", "note": "The source commentary labels يَوْمِ and develops it as an arriving judgment event; Zephaniah uses the cognate Hebrew יוֹם in its approaching “day of the Lord,” placing the judgment in a shared Semitic day formula."}
{"connection_id": "BC-52fe35ab62d08cc0f5b6", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.1.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "day of wrath and distress", "note": "The commentary reads divine anger as punitive retribution; Zephaniah describes the day as wrath, distress and ruin, specifying the judgment's felt consequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fbfa84ee6d7a5d963f89", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.1.15", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "day of wrath, distress, ruin, darkness", "note": "Zephaniah layers several “days” within the day of YHWH: wrath, distress, devastation, darkness. It is a close biblical realization of the commentary's severe day that bears many events within it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-564b1fd9f339f85cf0d8", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.1.18", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "silver and gold cannot deliver on the day of wrath", "note": "Zephaniah says neither silver nor gold can deliver on YHWH's day of wrath. It is a direct biblical counterpart to the commentary's claim that a final substitute payment is not accepted."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7152c07fa5f0cfd59743", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:1", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the righteous stand before their oppressors", "note": "The commentary connects rising to the final division of people; Wisdom says the righteous will stand with confidence before those who afflicted them, adding a reversal in which the formerly oppressed are vindicated."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ecf2738b2d0b57244631", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "erring from the way of truth", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the favored path from those who stray; the wicked in Wisdom's postmortem confession say they wandered from the way of truth, adding a scene where the astray recognize their error after the righteous are vindicated."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f838da0e5d9e38f2a353", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:7", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weary paths of lawlessness", "note": "The commentary contrasts a straight path with misguidance; the same confession says the wicked wearied themselves on paths of lawlessness, making the failed route a life of chosen conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf10dc04bfc0fa597372", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 6:3", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rulers examined by God", "note": "The commentary defines the malik branch as one with command and authority; Wisdom says rulers received sovereignty from God, who will examine their works, making earthly rule answerable to the final sovereign."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5f2b986957c2d5c21d6c", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 6:4", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unjust rulers are judged for not walking rightly", "note": "The commentary defines the malik branch as command over a community and connects the straight path to right conduct; Wisdom rebukes rulers for not ruling rightly or walking according to God's will, tying royal authority to a path by which it is judged."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d323d0797188073872ae", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 6:5", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "severe judgment for rulers", "note": "Wisdom warns that a severe judgment comes upon those in high places. It turns the commentary's kingly hierarchy back upon human rulers, who too face the sovereign Judge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76c1eff2fc15f95587d1", "ref": "b. Kiddushin 40b", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one deed tips the balance", "note": "The commentary's straight path is also a balance that does not tilt; Kiddushin says a person should view the world as evenly balanced and one commandment can tip it toward merit, turning ethical choice into a decisive measure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b58b9925d9830814e5ed", "ref": "b. Rosh Hashanah 16b", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "books opened on New Year", "note": "The commentary describes a day when deeds are accounted and outcomes differ; the Talmud says books are opened on Rosh Hashanah and distinguishes righteous, wicked and intermediate people, adding a Jewish liturgical calendar for judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8843467f021455bd43be", "ref": "b. Rosh Hashanah 16b", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "all pass before God; three books opened", "note": "The rabbinic account of Rosh Hashanah has all creatures pass before God and speaks of heavenly books for verdicts. It interprets judgment and record imagery as an annually enacted reckoning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f13b2ebd2b7eccc9038", "ref": "b. Shabbat 31a", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "first judgment question concerns honest business", "note": "The commentary develops debt, exchange and dishonest scales as ethical matters; the Talmud says a person is first asked at judgment whether business was conducted faithfully, bringing commercial honesty directly into the afterlife account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-58b34197748dde2674f9", "ref": "b. Shabbat 31a", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "“Did you conduct business faithfully?”", "note": "At heavenly judgment, faithful business dealing is named among the first questions asked. It directly carries the commentary's market-scale ethics into personal eschatological accountability."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e159dd4f875766e6b7e9", "ref": "m. Avot 3:16", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "credit, ledger, collectors", "note": "The commentary makes the appointed day the settlement of debt; Avot pictures the world as a shop with credit, an open ledger, and collectors exacting payment, an unusually close Jewish accounting image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-908aaadf5efedb688530", "ref": "m. Avot 4:22", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dead revived; living judged", "note": "The commentary links the q-w-m branch to resurrection and people standing before God; Avot says the dead will live again and the living will be judged, an especially compact Jewish formulation of the whole scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-96333bafce8b3f921581", "ref": "m. Avot 4:22", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no forgetfulness, favoritism or bribes", "note": "The commentary notes witness-memory failure and says an offered equivalent is rejected; Avot says the Judge has no forgetting, favoritism or acceptance of bribes and that all is according to reckoning, directly sharpening both details."}

